.\" The sections a manual page carries and an option's help cannot.
.\"
.\" csvdt's page is rendered from the same option definitions that parse a
.\" command line, which is what stops it describing options this build does
.\" not have. What that leaves out is everything belonging to the program
.\" rather than to one of its options: the environment it reads, the file it
.\" writes, what to reach for next. GNU coreutils fills the same gap with a
.\" per-page man/prog.x that help2man merges in; this is csvdt's one file of
.\" it, spliced in after the generated sections and before VERSION.
.\"
.\" Hand-written roff, so it is held to what the generated part is held to: a
.\" test renders the whole page under groff with every warning on and fails
.\" on a word of output, and another refuses a byte above 7-bit ASCII.
.\"
.\" What is written here is prose and cannot be generated, so the claims in it
.\" are asked of the program separately. ENVIRONMENT's is the one worth naming:
.\" no_conversion_but_local_reads_the_zone runs -u, -o, -d, -s and -r under
.\" eight settings of TZ and requires one answer from each, and requires -l to
.\" differ, since a build reading the zone nowhere would satisfy the first half
.\" and break the promise this section makes.
.\"
.\" FILES is enumerated rather than read: an option whose value --list-options
.\" spells 'file' is a file csvdt opens because it was told to, and
.\" what_the_manual_page_says_about_files_is_what_csvdt_does requires the
.\" section to name every one of them. This section called the spool below
.\" "the only file csvdt creates" for a whole release after --fill-log made
.\" that false, which is what enumeration is here to stop happening twice.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ
The zone
.B \-l/\-\-local
converts into. Unset, the system's configured zone is used.
.IP
A zone name behaves identically on every platform. A value holding POSIX
rules rather than a name, such as
.BR EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 ,
is honoured only on Unix; on Windows the system's zone is used instead. A
name the database compiled into this build does not know is refused rather
than answered, since falling through to UTC would report a plausible
timestamp for a zone nobody asked for.
.IP
No other conversion reads it.
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-d ,
.B \-s
and
.B \-r
work from the offset the record already carries, and so give the same
answer on any machine.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where
.B \-\-flexible=widest
puts the copy it makes when the input cannot be read twice. On Windows,
.B TMP
and then
.B TEMP
are read instead. Named here because the message about a temporary file
that cannot be created has to name the variable a reader is meant to set,
and the two families spell it differently.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/csvdt\-widest\-<pid>.csv
The only file csvdt creates unasked.
.B \-\-flexible=widest
reads its input twice, once to find the widest record and once to pad every
row out to it, so an input that cannot be rewound is copied here first: a
pipe, or standard input. A file argument is read twice directly and no copy
is made.
.IP
Created with mode 0600, since the copy holds the whole of an input that is
often a log, and removed when the run ends \(em including when the run is
interrupted. A numbered variant is tried when the name is taken, so a stale
copy left behind by a killed run cannot lock the mode out.
.TP
.B \-\-fill\-log
The destination this option names is the other file csvdt creates, and the
only one it creates because it was told to. An existing one is replaced
rather than added to, so a run's log is that run's and no other's, and a
destination that is the input, or where the output is going, is refused
before anything is opened.
.IP
It carries a line number, a record number, a field count and what was done,
for each record that did not match the width \(em and no field of the input,
which is why it is not given the mode above.
.SH NOTES
Output goes to standard output and no file is edited in place. A redirection
onto the input is refused rather than obeyed: a shell empties the file
before csvdt is started, so the data is gone before it could be read. Write
another file and move it into place.
.PP
Linux is the intended platform: it is what csvdt is developed and packaged
on, and what the differential checks are run against before a release.
macOS and Windows are supported on a best-effort basis \-\- built and tested
on every commit, and worth a report, but given less attention and less
coverage. Where something cannot be had on one of them it is said so
rather than left to be found out.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iconv (1)
converts an input csvdt refuses to read; it requires UTF\-8.
.BR date (1)
does these conversions for one timestamp rather than a column of them.
.BR tzselect (1)
finds the name of a zone to put in
.BR TZ .
.PP
The README carries what this page does not: how to install it, how to wrap
it, and the reasoning behind the choices. It is at
https://github.com/YardQuit/csvdt
